# The other side of `pty_terminate_and_signal_external_job.crush`: once `fg` has brought a
# backgrounded external job back into the foreground, it *is* the foreground job again,
# so its process dying from a signal is an ordinary foreground death (like Ctrl-C) and
# must not leave a one-time "Terminated" row behind in `jobs` (see `cmd.rs`'s `Signaled`
# arm). Guards against "not holding the foreground" being sticky: `fg` must reset it
# (see `JobData::backgrounded`).
#
# KNOWN GAP, not yet closed like the sibling pty tests' sleeps: `fg 0` blocks the
# nested session's own prompt until the job it resumed actually finishes, so there is
# no prompt (counted or otherwise) to synchronize on between submitting it and sending
# the signal that lets it finish -- and nothing else observable from outside this pty
# (no `jobs` column, no other query) reveals "has `fg`'s dispatch actually reached
# `GlobalState::set_backgrounded` yet", which is the specific internal state this
# test's own correctness guarantee depends on. Confirmed directly: removing this delay
# entirely made the check below fail *every* time (5/5), not occasionally -- sending
# the signal immediately after writing "fg 0\n" reliably outraces the nested session's
# own dispatch of that line, landing while the job is still considered backgrounded
# and producing a spurious `Terminated` row. This is the same shape of problem as the
# Ctrl-Z/Ctrl-C pty tests' settle delays (signaling a job from outside crush's own
# control flow, with nothing to synchronize on): left as a fixed delay pending that
# same investigation, rather than converted to a guess disguised as something more
# precise.
source tests/pty_test_helpers.crush
$t := $(pty_spawn)
$n := $(pty_enable_counted_prompt $t)

$n = $(pty_run $t $n "cmd sleep 7434 &")
$pid := $(pty_child_pid $t ^(.*sleep))
$t:write "fg 0\n"
sleep 800ms
host:signal $pid signal=SIGKILL
$n = ($n + 1)
$t:wait_for $("PROMPT {}#":format $n)

$n = $(pty_run $t $n "$n := $(jobs | where {($status == \"Terminated\")} | count)")
$t:write "echo $(\"FG_SIG_CHECK_{}\":format $n)\n"
$t:wait_for "FG_SIG_CHECK_0"

$t:close
